---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Note from '../components/case/Note.astro';
import '../styles/prose.css';
---

<Base
  title="This site"
  description="Why the first version of this portfolio was retired, and how this one is built: a power switch, offline reading, a strict CSP, and about 5 KB of JavaScript."
>
  <article class="page colophon">
    <header class="colophon-head">
      <p class="label">This site</p>
      <h1 class="colophon-title">The portfolio is a case study too.</h1>
      <p class="colophon-lead">
        If I say I design for failure conditions, the site should work under them. Here's what I
        changed, and why.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose colophon-body">
      <h2 id="what-died">What I retired</h2>
      <p>
        The first version was <a href="/v1/">Nostalgia OS</a>: choose Windows Vista or Mac OS X, watch it
        boot, then open desktop windows to find the work. It had a real window manager and an event bus,
        and I was proud of the engineering.
      </p>
      <p>It also got in the way of everything that mattered:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The work sat behind a choice, a boot sequence and at least three clicks.</li>
        <li>It borrowed Microsoft's and Apple's design languages instead of showing mine.</li>
        <li>It was barely usable on a phone, and search engines saw an empty page.</li>
        <li>It said "I'm nostalgic", when my work is about infrastructure that doesn't exist yet.</li>
      </ul>
      <Note kind="decision">
        Keep the one good idea from v1: the site itself should prove something. Retire the rest.
      </Note>
      <p>It's still online and playable, because deleting it would hide the iteration.</p>

      <h2 id="idea">The idea</h2>
      <p>
        My projects share a way of working more than a stack: design for the moment things go wrong,
        write down what you decided, and make the important rules impossible to break. So this site is a
        working ledger: paper, ink, and one vermilion stamp that only marks decisions, status and where
        you are. Nothing is decorative.
      </p>

      <h2 id="power">The power switch</h2>
      <p>
        In Zimbabwe, load-shedding (scheduled power cuts) is a design constraint. My systems are built
        for it, so this site has a <strong>Power</strong> switch in the header. Turn it off and:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Web fonts, colour and motion drop away.</li>
        <li>Every diagram is replaced by its written version, the same text screen readers always get.</li>
        <li>
          The page measures itself with the browser's Resource Timing data and tells you how much of it you
          actually needed to read it.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If your browser sends <code>Save-Data</code>, the site starts with the power off and says why. With
        the power off, fonts are never requested at all: even the preload is decided before first paint.
      </p>
      <p>
        Separately, a service worker keeps every page you open, and quietly saves the pages it links to when
        your browser is idle. Lose your connection and the site keeps working. That part isn't a
        simulation.
      </p>

      <h2 id="budgets">Budgets, measured</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>What</th><th>Budget</th><th>Actual</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>JavaScript, whole site</td><td>&lt; 60 KB</td><td>≈ 5 KB, inlined and hash-pinned</td></tr>
          <tr><td>CSS, gzipped</td><td>&lt; 30 KB</td><td>≈ 8 KB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Homepage HTML, gzipped</td><td>n/a</td><td>≈ 13 KB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fonts</td><td>2 families</td><td>≈ 170 KB, the one deliberate spend, skipped with the power off</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Images</td><td>n/a</td><td>0. Every diagram is SVG or HTML drawn from real numbers</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="craft">What's underneath</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Astro</strong> renders every page to static HTML. Case studies are typed MDX with a schema,
          so a project can't be published without its role, status and evidence.
        </li>
        <li>
          The "What keeps coming back" table on the homepage is <em>generated</em> from the case studies,
          so it can't disagree with them.
        </li>
        <li>
          A strict Content Security Policy with SHA-256 hashes for every script and style. No
          <code>unsafe-inline</code> and no inline style attributes.
        </li>
        <li>
          Semantic landmarks, a skip link, visible focus everywhere, a real <code>role="switch"</code>,
          reduced-motion support, and light and dark themes that follow your system.
        </li>
        <li>Unit tests for the logic; browser tests for the switch, keyboard use and every breakpoint.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="type">Type and colour</h2>
      <p>
        <em>Newsreader</em> for reading and headlines: a serif built for long-form text on screens.
        <em>Schibsted Grotesk</em> for labels and numbers. It was drawn for a newspaper, which suits a site
        that reads like a record. The palette is warm paper, blue-black ink, and a vermilion taken from the
        rubber stamps on council forms.
      </p>

      <h2 id="next">What I'd still improve</h2>
      <p>
        Real photographs from the field: the auction yard, a building site mid-inspection. They're worth
        more than any diagram, and I don't have good ones yet. Until I do, the diagrams stay honest rather
        than illustrative.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .colophon-head {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 4vw, 5rem) var(--space-12);
  }

  .colophon-title {
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 1.4rem + 3.2vw, 4.25rem);
    font-weight: 380;
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    max-width: 16ch;
  }

  .colophon-lead {
    font-size: var(--text-lead);
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    max-width: 34rem;
  }

  .colophon-body {
    --margin-col: 15rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  @media (min-width: 72rem) {
    .colophon-body {
      margin-left: calc(12rem + var(--space-12));
    }
  }
</style>
